.TH HD-KERNEL-DESKTOP 7 "November 2026" "hd-kernel-desktop" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-kernel-desktop \- the HyprDebian kernel for desktops and laptops
.SH DESCRIPTION
The package
.B hd-kernel-desktop
depends on the most recent HyprDebian kernel for desktops of the suite and its
ZFS module, so that they are installed and upgraded with it:
.TP
.BI linux-image- release
The kernel of
.BR hd-kernel (7):
the kernel of Debian, with the patches and the config of Debian, built by the
build server of HyprDebian, but with only the modules that a desktop or laptop
needs.
.TP
.BI zfs-modules- release
The ZFS module of the
.B zfs-linux
of Debian, built against that kernel, in
.IR /lib/modules/ release /extra/zfs .
It provides
.BR zfs-modules ,
which
.B zfs-initramfs
takes in place of
.BR zfs-dkms .
.PP
What the config of Debian builds into the kernel stays, and so do the modules
that are not drivers: the file systems, the network protocols and netfilter,
with wireless and Bluetooth, crypto, and kvm, for virtual machines. Of the
drivers only these stay:
.IP \(bu 2
those of the hardware of desktops and laptops: the graphics of AMD, Intel and
Nvidia; HDA sound; SATA and NVMe disks, SD cards and cd-roms; the network
cards of Intel, Realtek, Aquantia, Atheros, Broadcom, Mellanox ConnectX and
Brocade, with Fibre Channel; the Wi-Fi of Intel, Realtek, MediaTek and
Qualcomm Atheros; the sensors, memory errors, watchdogs and SMBus of the
chipsets of AMD and Intel; the platform drivers of Asus, Gigabyte and Dell;
FireWire; printers on the parallel port;
.IP \(bu 2
every driver of what people plug in: USB, HID and input, Bluetooth, webcams,
USB sound and USB network adapters and tethering;
.IP \(bu 2
those of software: loop, zram and nbd, device mapper and RAID, and the network
devices of containers, virtual machines, VPNs and bonds, such as tun, veth,
macvlan, vxlan, wireguard and vhost.
.PP
The headers of each kernel, for DKMS, are in
.BI linux-headers- release\fR,
which no package depends on; to build modules for the kernel that runs:
.PP
.RS
sudo apt install linux-headers-$(uname -r)
.RE
.PP
The release is
.IB x.y.z +debNN-hddesktop id -amd64\fR,
such as
.BR 7.2.12+deb14-hddesktop1a2b3c-amd64 ,
where
.I id
comes from the versions of
.BR linux ,
.B zfs-linux
and
.BR hd-kernel-desktop .
A new build has a new release, so it installs next to the kernel that runs.
.PP
Every build signs the modules of the kernel and the ZFS module with a key it
makes for the build and then throws away; the kernel trusts that key and no
other for its modules.
.SH SWITCHING
To switch a desktop or laptop to the HyprDebian kernel for desktops:
.PP
.RS
sudo apt install hd-kernel-desktop
.RE
.PP
This removes the other HyprDebian kernels,
.BR hd-kernel ,
.B hd-kernel-vm
and
.BR hd-kernel-server ,
and
.BR linux-image-amd64 ,
.B linux-headers-amd64
and
.BR linux-base-amd64 ,
so the other kernels get no more upgrades.
After the installation the bootloader shows the new kernel at the top of its
menu, with
.B -hd
in its name, see
.BR hd-bootloader-update (8).
Before the system boots it, check that the kernel has the drivers of its
graphics, disks and network: a driver that is not in
.IR /lib/modules/ release
is not in the kernel.
Once it boots, the other kernels can be purged, as
.BR hd-kernel (7)
describes for the kernels of Debian.
.PP
To switch back to the kernel of
.BR hd-kernel ,
or to that of Debian:
.PP
.RS
sudo apt install hd-kernel
.br
sudo apt install linux-image-amd64 linux-headers-amd64
.RE
.PP
Either removes
.BR hd-kernel-desktop ;
its kernels stay until they are purged.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR /boot/vmlinuz- release
.TQ
.IR /lib/modules/ release
The kernel and its modules.
.TP
.IR /lib/modules/ release /extra/zfs
The ZFS module.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd-bootloader-update (8),
.BR hd-buildserver (8),
.BR hd-kernel (7),
.BR hd-kernel-server (7),
.BR hd-kernel-vm (7),
.BR zfs (4)
